<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-practice-calibration">
  <title>Coding Methodology</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain why training is staged and why access changes across milestones.</li>
      <li>Distinguish development, calibration, supervised coding, production coding, and held-out validation.</li>
      <li>Use observable competencies to judge readiness for independent work.</li>
      <li>Target feedback and remediation at the earliest failed decision.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Researcher development serves two connected purposes. It prepares coders to
      apply the Episode Model and coding workflow consistently, and it tests whether
      the project's rules, examples, codebook, and worksheets are sufficiently clear
      to support reproducible human coding.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter explains the methodology of the sequence. Milestone worksheets
      administer the work: they assign readings and packets, define permitted and
      prohibited materials, specify fields and deliverables, and record advancement
      decisions. The handbook remains the canonical source for definitions, rules,
      examples, and methodological rationale.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-purpose">
    <title>Why the Training Sequence Exists</title>

    <p>
      The project is developing both a reliable human coding procedure and a
      benchmark against which the computational pipeline can later be evaluated.
      Training must therefore produce more than agreement after discussion. It must
      show whether coders can make defensible first-pass decisions independently,
      preserve uncertainty, and apply frozen materials consistently.
    </p>

    <p>
      Recurring disagreement may indicate coder error, but it may also reveal an
      ambiguous response, overlapping code definitions, an incomplete question frame,
      or an unstated policy. Training records are development data for the method.
      They should identify where the framework is stable and where revision is needed
      before formal validation begins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-sequence">
    <title>Milestone Overview</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-practice-training-sequence">
      <title>Methodological purpose of each researcher milestone.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="25%"/>
        <col width="75%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Milestone</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Methodological purpose</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Onboarding</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Learn the framework and evidence boundary with guidance and feedback.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Blind pass</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Preserve unaided screening, segmentation, and episode extraction.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Codebook-assisted pass</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Apply formal categories and normative mathematics without overwriting the blind record.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Calibration</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Assess independent reproducibility and diagnose the earliest source of disagreement.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Supervised coding</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Test transfer from constructed practice to approved project responses.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Production coding</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Produce auditable research records under frozen materials and documented review rules.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Held-out validation</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Evaluate the frozen human method and computational pipeline without revising them from the evaluation cases.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Advancement is sequential but not automatic. A completed worksheet supplies
      evidence for review; it does not by itself authorize the next level of access or
      independent project coding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-independence">
    <title>Independence and Anchoring Controls</title>

    <p>
      Independence controls protect the evidentiary value of the first pass. Blind
      records are locked before codebook-assisted work begins. Codebook access is
      delayed when the milestone is intended to measure unaided extraction. Machine
      proposals and other coders' records remain hidden until the authorized review or
      comparison stage.
    </p>

    <p>
      These restrictions are methodological controls rather than general prohibitions.
      The assigned worksheet states exactly what may be consulted. A later assisted
      pass may add codes, correctness, coverage, or review decisions, but it must
      preserve the earlier record so that anchoring, revision, and disagreement can be
      studied.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-calibration">
    <title>Calibration and Adjudication</title>

    <p>
      Calibration asks whether coders can apply the same frozen materials
      independently. Each coder completes the full first-pass record before 
      comparison. Discussion that occurs before the independent records are
      complete cannot be used as evidence of reproducibility.
    </p>

    <p>
      Calibration disagreements are classified at the earliest workflow stage where
      the records diverge. Coders state there reasoning referencing the coding 
      materials, and adjudicate only when one reading is better supported. When the 
      evidence or current rules do not determine a unique answer, the unresolved 
      status remains visible.
    </p>

    <p>
      Calibration is development data. It may be used to revise training materials,
      codebook definitions, examples, vocabulary, or policy before the method is
      frozen. Held-out validation data must not be used for that purpose. If a
      validation case exposes a serious flaw, the project must version the change and
      decide explicitly whether the affected evaluation must be restarted or reported
      separately.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-readiness">
    <title>Readiness</title>

    <p>
      Readiness does not mean that a coder always agrees with an expert or never
      encounters uncertainty. It means that the coder can apply the current protocol
      independently, explain decisions from visible evidence, and identify where the
      protocol does not determine a unique answer.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-practice-readiness">
      <title>Observable competencies required for independent coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="30%"/>
        <col width="45%"/>
        <col width="25%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p>Competency</p></cell><cell><p>Observable evidence</p></cell><cell><p>Common failure</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Framework boundary</p></cell><cell><p>Describes visible reasoning without claiming a complete conception.</p></cell><cell><p>Attributes unstated knowledge or belief.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Screening</p></cell><cell><p>Separates codability from correctness and preserves uncertain cases.</p></cell><cell><p>Includes only correct answers or excludes unfamiliar language.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Episode extraction</p></cell><cell><p>Records complete and incomplete episodes without repair.</p></cell><cell><p>Supplies a missing criterion, link, or judgment.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Segmentation</p></cell><cell><p>Identifies chained, independent, and nested structures and applies the current retention policy.</p></cell><cell><p>Collapses reasoning without recording the alternative.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Matching</p></cell><cell><p>Uses definitions, exclusions, and question-specific links.</p></cell><cell><p>Codes from isolated keywords.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Correctness and coverage</p></cell><cell><p>Evaluates criterion truth, link validity, and goal coverage separately.</p></cell><cell><p>Lets final-answer correctness determine every annotation.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Diagnostic judgment</p></cell><cell><p>Distinguishes OOV, no match, ambiguity, and policy gaps.</p></cell><cell><p>Forces all cases into an existing code.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Documentation</p></cell><cell><p>Writes concise evidence-based rationales and records governing versions.</p></cell><cell><p>Uses vague impressions or leaves low-confidence decisions unexplained.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>Independent work</p></cell><cell><p>Completes the first pass before comparison and escalates responsibly.</p></cell><cell><p>Negotiates uncertain decisions during the independent pass.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-feedback">
    <title>Feedback and Remediation</title>

    <p>
      Feedback should identify the earliest decision that failed. A wrong final code
      may originate in screening, segmentation, component interpretation, matching, or
      use of the normative reference. Remediation should target that decision rather
      than repeat the entire sequence by default.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-practice-remediation">
      <title>Examples of targeted remediation.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="28%"/>
        <col width="39%"/>
        <col width="33%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell><p>Recurring difficulty</p></cell><cell><p>Likely source</p></cell><cell><p>Remediation</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Bare answers receive codes.</p></cell><cell><p>Final-answer halo effect.</p></cell><cell><p>Use paired bare-answer and justified-answer screening cases.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Missing links are invented.</p></cell><cell><p>Confusion between interpretation and repair.</p></cell><cell><p>Practice verbatim fields, minimal links, and absent-link cases.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Multi-step responses become one row.</p></cell><cell><p>Weak segmentation or an unresolved nesting policy.</p></cell><cell><p>Annotate judgment transitions and compare alternative segmentations.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Codes follow keywords.</p></cell><cell><p>Definitions and exclusions are not being applied.</p></cell><cell><p>Use near-miss sets containing the same words in different inferential roles.</p></cell></row>
        <row><cell><p>Correctness and coverage are conflated.</p></cell><cell><p>Response-level grading habits.</p></cell><cell><p>Score local episodes first, then assess prompt goals separately.</p></cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><p>Many coders fail on the same case.</p></cell><cell><p>A material or policy defect may be more likely than individual error.</p></cell><cell><p>Review the definition, question frame, examples, and protocol before retraining coders.</p></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Test a proposed revision on the case that exposed the problem, at least two
      similar cases, and at least one near miss. Adopt substantive changes through the
      project's versioned revision process before formal validation materials are
      frozen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-practice-worksheet-index">
    <title>Worksheet Index</title>

    <p>
      The worksheet sequence authorizes specific work and access. The first three
      worksheets are present in the current book; later worksheets remain to be
      drafted before the corresponding operational material is removed from this
      chapter.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-practice-worksheet-index">
      <title>Current milestone worksheets.</title>
      <tabular top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
        <col width="60%" halign="left"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Worksheet</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What it authorizes</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>01-onboarding</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Guided orientation and constructed practice.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>02-blind-pass</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Independent screening and episode extraction under blind access controls.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>03-codebook-pass</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Codebook matching, correctness, coverage, and assisted-record preservation.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>04-calibration</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Independent comparison, disagreement records, adjudication, and calibration decisions.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p><c>05-supervised-coding</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Transfer to approved project responses under research-lead review.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p><c>06-production-coding</c></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Production batch rules, quality checks, review sampling, and authorization boundaries.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The goal of researcher development is not rapid agreement. It is independent,
      reproducible judgment supported by visible evidence. A ready coder can apply the
      workflow, document uncertainty, learn from disagreement, and recognize when the
      analytic materials rather than the response or coder require revision.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>
